# Erdős Problem #99 — coefficient-27 near-endpoint activation

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependency branch:** `research/c2-cusp35-sol-20260914`  
**Claim class:** proof-only structural refinement of the coefficient-27 scalar endpoint  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The coefficient-27 scalar floor does more than exclude
`t< C146+0.00452`. Immediately above that floor it forces the outer detour to use
almost all of its allowed topology budget. The unused detour slack then controls the
complete Woodall/mixed-rigidity defect

\[
\kappa={d_H+s_Z\over D}.
\]

This note quantifies that activation on the narrow band

\[
\boxed{
C_{146}+0.00452\le t\le C_{146}+0.00460.
}
\tag{A27.1}
\]

Every survivor in this band satisfies

\[
\boxed{0.00839<\delta\le0.00860,}
\tag{A27.2}
\]

\[
\boxed{\kappa<0.00084,}
\tag{A27.3}
\]

and consequently the loaded diameter graph and bounded defect network are far closer
to their extremal forms than the coarse bound `kappa<=2R(t)` suggests.

## 1. Scalar feasibility forces `delta>0.00839`

Retain the coefficient-27 scalar function

\[
F_t^{(27)}(\delta)=
10t-5\pi-5\delta
+\left(27(\pi+\delta)^2\delta\right)^{1/3}
-{8\Delta_2\over {9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta}.
\tag{A27.4}
\]

The previous theorem proves that `F_t^(27)` is increasing in both `t` and `delta`
through the relevant small-detour domain. The present certificate extends the
`delta`-derivative check through `delta=0.00860` with the explicit lower bound

\[
{\partial F_t^{(27)}\over\partial\delta}>42.
\tag{A27.5}
\]

Set

\[
t_{60}=C_{146}+0.00460,
\qquad \delta_0=0.00839.
\]

Outward rational arithmetic gives

\[
\boxed{F_{t_{60}}^{(27)}(\delta_0)<-2.5\times10^{-4}.}
\tag{A27.6}
\]

Therefore if `t<=t60` and `delta<=delta0`, monotonicity gives

\[
F_t^{(27)}(\delta)
\le F_{t60}^{(27)}(\delta_0)<0,
\]

contradicting scalar feasibility. This proves the lower half of (A27.2).

The atomic feasibility interval gives

\[
\delta\le\delta_{max}(t)
={ {9\over2}(t-C_{146})-0.0035\over2}.
\]

At `t=t60`,

\[
\delta_{max}=0.00860,
\]

proving the upper half of (A27.2). The full interval remains inside the disjoint-three-cusp regime.

## 2. Detour activation collapses the topology defect

The low-`t` topology identity is

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}
\le R(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0,
\qquad
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0035.
\tag{A27.7}
\]

At `t=t60`,

\[
R(t_{60})=4.5(0.00460)-0.0035=0.01720.
\]

Using `delta>0.00839`,

\[
\kappa
\le2R(t)-4\delta
<2(0.01720)-4(0.00839)
=0.00084.
\]

This proves (A27.3).

Equivalently, in terms of unused detour slack,

\[
\boxed{
\kappa\le4\bigl(\delta_{max}(t)-\delta\bigr).
}
\tag{A27.8}
\]

Thus the scalar inequality has activated almost the entire topology budget into actual
metric detour.

## 3. The loaded diameter graph is `>99.9539%` extremal

The exact diameter-graph identities are

\[
{q\over D}=t+\kappa,
\qquad
{h\over D}=t+{s_Z\over D}\ge t.
\]

Using the lower endpoint `t35=C146+0.00452` and (A27.3),

\[
\boxed{
{h\over q}
\ge {t_{35}\over t_{35}+0.00084}
>0.99953969-o(1).
}
\tag{A27.9}
\]

If the Woodall graph is in the caterpillar-forest branch, then `d_H<=k`, so every
nonempty forest satisfies

\[
\boxed{
{q\over d_H}
\ge {t_{35}\over0.00084}
>2171.46-o(1).
}
\tag{A27.10}
\]

Thus the forest branch has only macroscopic caterpillar components; the alternative is
the canonical connected odd-unicyclic thrackle.

## 4. Almost every exceptional face is a pentagon

Write

\[
\varphi={f\over D},
\qquad
\chi={Q\over D}.
\]

The exact low-`t` identities give

\[
\varphi\ge {C_0\over2}-{t\over4}+{\kappa\over4},
\tag{A27.11}
\]

and

\[
\chi-\varphi
\le {9t\over4}-\pi-{C_0\over2}-\delta-{\kappa\over4}.
\tag{A27.12}
\]

At `t=t60`, with `delta>0.00839` and `kappa>=0`,

\[
\boxed{
{Q-f\over D}<0.000211+o(1).
}
\tag{A27.13}
\]

while

\[
\boxed{
{f\over D}\ge0.498034422369096\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{A27.14}
\]

Since `p_5>=2f-Q`,

\[
\boxed{
{p_5\over D}
\ge0.497824422369097\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{A27.15}
\]

So fewer than about `0.043%` of bounded exceptional faces can have degree at least six.
Their total terminal occurrence count obeys

\[
\boxed{
{B_{\ge6}\over D}<0.001266+o(1).
}
\tag{A27.16}
\]

## 5. Pentagon closure rank: the `kappa` loss cancels

For the pentagon set `P_5`, the audited closure-rank theorem gives

\[
\operatorname{rank}C_{P_5}
\ge2p_5-\sigma_C,
\qquad
\sigma_C\le s_Z.
\tag{A27.17}
\]

The sharper pentagon formula before discarding nonnegative terms is

\[
{p_5\over D}
\ge \pi+C_0-{5t\over2}+\delta+{\kappa\over2}-o(1).
\tag{A27.18}
\]

Therefore

\[
{\operatorname{rank}C_{P_5}\over D}
\ge
2\pi+2C_0-5t+2\delta
+\kappa-{s_Z\over D}-o(1).
\]

But

\[
\kappa-{s_Z\over D}={d_H\over D}\ge0.
\]

Hence the full topology defect cancels rather than being paid pessimistically:

\[
\boxed{
{\operatorname{rank}C_{P_5}\over D}
\ge2\pi+2C_0-5t+2\delta-o(1).
}
\tag{A27.19}
\]

At the activated band endpoint this yields

\[
\boxed{
{\operatorname{rank}C_{P_5}\over D}
>0.995648844738194-o(1).
}
\tag{A27.20}
\]

This cancellation is stronger than first setting `kappa=0` in the pentagon count and
then subtracting a separate worst-case stress corank.

## 6. The stopped-train count is almost pinned

Let

\[
B=Q+4f
\]

be the bounded-face terminal occurrence count. Since `Q>=f`, (A27.14) gives

\[
{B\over D}\ge5{f\over D}
\ge2.490172111845481\ldots-o(1).
\tag{A27.21}
\]

The Section-171 endpoint capacity gives

\[
{B\over D}\le10t-5\pi-5\delta,
\]

so in the activated band

\[
\boxed{
2.490172111845481\ldots
\le {B\over D}
<2.491222111845478\ldots
}
\tag{A27.22}
\]

up to `o(1)`.

Since the outer occurrence count is `b/D=pi+delta` and `2T=b+B`,

\[
\boxed{
2.820077382717637\ldots
\le {T\over D}
<2.820602382717636\ldots
}
\tag{A27.23}
\]

up to `o(1)`. The admissible first-order train-count band has width only

\[
0.000525D.
\]

## 7. More outer-to-outer bracing than the coarse bound sees

The endpoint identities

\[
b=2L_{OO}+L_{OB},
\qquad
B=L_{OB}+2L_{BB}
\]

give

\[
L_{OO}\ge{b-B\over2}.
\]

Using the activated lower detour and the Section-171 upper bound for `B`,

\[
{L_{OO}\over D}
\ge3\pi+3\delta-5t-o(1).
\]

Therefore throughout the band

\[
\boxed{
{L_{OO}\over D}
>0.329380270872158-o(1).
}
\tag{A27.24}
\]

Every one of these cuts remains two-sidedly braced by actual diameter edges.

## 8. The tooth / long-side burden recovers its stronger size

The exact physical boundary inequality is

\[
{N_2+2L_{>2}\over D}
\ge \pi-t-\kappa-\delta-o(1).
\tag{A27.25}
\]

Using (A27.7),

\[
\kappa+\delta
\le2R(t)-3\delta.
\]

The worst point in the activated band is `t=t60`, `delta=0.00839`, giving

\[
\boxed{
{N_2+2L_{>2}\over D}
>1.308249115610349-o(1).
}
\tag{A27.26}
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{
{1\over D}\max\{N_2,L_{>2}\}
>0.436083038536783-o(1).
}
\tag{A27.27}
\]

On the tooth branch choose the shallowness threshold `eta=0.065`, which remains below
the geometric limit `2(1-cos(pi/12))`. Since `delta<=0.00860`, the shallow-tooth theorem gives more than `0.3037D` shallow teeth and therefore more than

\[
\boxed{0.0759D-o(D)}
\]

distinct inward penetration contacts, each with inward normal component greater than
`0.711`.

## 9. Frontier effect

A survivor in the first `8e-5`-wide `t` band above the new scalar floor must now realize
all of the following simultaneously:

```text
0.00839 < outer detour / D <= 0.00860
k / D < 0.00084
loaded diameter graph >99.9539% of Woodall edge density
forest components, if present, average >2171 loaded vertices
Q-f < 0.000211 D
p_5 > 0.497824 D
pentagon closure rank >0.995648 D
total stopped trains in a width-0.000525 D band
outer-to-outer trains >0.329380 D
max(two-edge teeth, long-side mass) >0.436083 D
```

This is a materially narrower structural target than the prior coarse low-`t` package.
The next closure step should exploit simultaneous near-equality, not another scalar
constant: either classify the almost extremal Woodall graph against the almost-full
pentagon closure matrix, or show that the activated outer-tooth/long-side population
cannot coexist with that near-equality.

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_cusp27_near_endpoint_activation.py
```

for the scalar-negativity, monotonicity, cusp-regime, and topology-slack certificates.

**Disposition:** proof-only near-endpoint structural refinement. C-II and Level I remain open.
